WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.930 --> 00:00:06.930
Hey, Wisecrack.

00:00:06.930 --> 00:00:07.930
Jared here.

00:00:07.930 --> 00:00:11.400
Full disclosure: this video was made possible
by the people over at VRV.

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:15.800
We’ve been fans of the Mondo show Happy
Tree Friends for a while, so when VRV reached

00:00:15.800 --> 00:00:20.680
out and asked us to check out the new Mondo
show Gary and His Demons streaming exclusively

00:00:20.680 --> 00:00:22.850
on VRV, we were pretty excited.

00:00:22.850 --> 00:00:26.870
And five episodes in, we couldn’t help but
notice a lot of similarities to two other

00:00:26.870 --> 00:00:32.279
Wisecrack favorites: Archer, and this show
that we’ve mentioned once or twice before

00:00:32.279 --> 00:00:33.610
— don’t hate.

00:00:33.610 --> 00:00:38.570
One particular moment stood out to us as particularly
Zeitgeisty: “When it comes to murdering

00:00:38.570 --> 00:00:40.920
people, I guess you could say…”

00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:42.160
“You’re hooked.”

00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:43.160
“What?

00:00:43.160 --> 00:00:44.649
Wha-What’d you say?”

00:00:44.649 --> 00:00:47.329
“I guess you could say you're hooked, that's
what you were going to say."

00:00:47.329 --> 00:00:49.420
“That wasn't what I was going to say" “Pretty
sure it is.”

00:00:49.420 --> 00:00:50.420
"No, it's not."

00:00:50.420 --> 00:00:52.150
"Your whole personality is hooks, man."

00:00:52.150 --> 00:00:54.469
"There's other hook things you can say!"

00:00:54.469 --> 00:00:55.469
“Alright.”

00:00:55.469 --> 00:00:58.180
Which reminded us of this — “Cool runnings,
man.”

00:00:58.180 --> 00:01:01.770
“God, would you give it a rest?

00:01:01.770 --> 00:01:04.040
Uh… Damn, I had something for this.”

00:01:04.040 --> 00:01:08.090
and this — “I guess I did the butler,
haha!

00:01:08.090 --> 00:01:09.730
Does… does that scan?”

00:01:09.730 --> 00:01:10.730
“Oh, I…

00:01:10.730 --> 00:01:11.780
I get it.”

00:01:11.780 --> 00:01:17.090
This subversion of hero one liners got us
thinking: why is this kind of humor so prevalent

00:01:17.090 --> 00:01:18.090
these days?

00:01:18.090 --> 00:01:22.860
Is there something more going on here that
says something about our modern social condition?

00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:26.540
And as we watched more, we realized that was
just the tip of the iceberg.

00:01:26.540 --> 00:01:32.119
So, if you haven’t seen Gary and His Demons
yet, check out vrv.co/wisecrack for a 30-day

00:01:32.119 --> 00:01:37.460
free trial, and join us, as we impart on a
time-honored Wisecrack tradition: over-explaining

00:01:37.460 --> 00:01:40.080
a joke for the sake of social commentary.

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:45.200
One thing we’ve noticed lately is a particular
trend in comedy: subjecting the fantastic

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:46.490
to the mundane.

00:01:46.490 --> 00:01:50.740
In Rick and Morty, the fanciful fairy tale
of Jack and the Beanstalk is taken down a

00:01:50.740 --> 00:01:53.829
peg, when the narrative becomes mired in legal
proceedings.

00:01:53.829 --> 00:01:58.810
The horrifying Freddy Krueger has the same
marital woes and performance anxiety as your

00:01:58.810 --> 00:02:03.909
average shlub, and even the post-apocalypse
still has suburban domestic spats.

00:02:03.909 --> 00:02:05.210
“Ding, ding, ding!

00:02:05.210 --> 00:02:06.210
Wow!

00:02:06.210 --> 00:02:07.210
Everybody hear that?”

00:02:07.210 --> 00:02:11.620
In Archer, the secret agent fantasy of blowing
sh*t up and womanizing is subjected to the

00:02:11.620 --> 00:02:16.840
realities of expense reports, — “You've
got some serious discrepancies in your account.”

00:02:16.840 --> 00:02:17.840
“No, Cyril.”

00:02:17.840 --> 00:02:21.109
“I’m sure you wouldn't use operational
funds for personal expenses.”

00:02:21.109 --> 00:02:28.559
— HR, — “Most of my job’s dealing
with sexual harassment complaints against

00:02:28.559 --> 00:02:31.919
Mr. Archer.” — and hearing loss — "What
the sh*t, Barry?"

00:02:31.919 --> 00:02:32.919
"What?

00:02:32.919 --> 00:02:33.919
We don't have any peppers."

00:02:33.919 --> 00:02:34.919
"Or earplugs, you idiot.

00:02:34.919 --> 00:02:36.980
You know how bad that is for your eardrums!?"

00:02:36.980 --> 00:02:37.980
“What?!”

00:02:37.980 --> 00:02:42.540
Gary and His Demons does the same, subjecting
demon-hunting to the mundanities of office

00:02:42.540 --> 00:02:43.950
life and sock puppets.

00:02:43.950 --> 00:02:48.400
“You’d be comfortable talking to my friend,
Sock Guy.”

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:49.400
“What?”

00:02:49.400 --> 00:02:51.219
“Hi, Sock Guy.”

00:02:51.219 --> 00:02:53.569
Why does this comedy work so well?

00:02:53.569 --> 00:02:54.659
And why now?

00:02:54.659 --> 00:02:58.260
Or, at least for adult cartoon viewers between
the ages of 18-34.

00:02:58.260 --> 00:03:06.069
Well, to be overly dramatic: one could say
it’s because OUR DREAMS ARE DEAD.

00:03:06.069 --> 00:03:07.069
Still with me?

00:03:07.069 --> 00:03:08.069
Good.

00:03:08.069 --> 00:03:11.669
Because to understand why, we’re gonna have
to dive in to the philosophy of humor.

00:03:11.669 --> 00:03:16.120
There are a few major theories of humor out
there, but one that popped up only a few hundred

00:03:16.120 --> 00:03:21.340
years ago is “incongruity” - basically
the idea that humor arises from the discrepancy

00:03:21.340 --> 00:03:23.699
between our expectations and reality.

00:03:23.699 --> 00:03:26.900
I mean, what is a punch-line if not the subversion
of our expectations?

00:03:26.900 --> 00:03:31.650
“Remember, we used to smoke pot and then
talk about how one day maybe we could on Conan

00:03:31.650 --> 00:03:32.650
together?”

00:03:32.650 --> 00:03:34.650
“Yeah, that was yesterday.”

00:03:34.650 --> 00:03:38.809
But there’s one philosopher who takes this
further, arguing that humor arises from the

00:03:38.809 --> 00:03:43.839
incongruity between our notion of ourselves
as humans, and the subversion of that notion

00:03:43.839 --> 00:03:46.620
by the mechanical nature of our lives.

00:03:46.620 --> 00:03:51.120
While this theory is a bit antiquated, we
can use it to help us understand humor today.

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:56.529
In 1900, philosopher Henri Bergson theorized
that the source of humor was the “mechanical

00:03:56.529 --> 00:03:58.650
encrusted upon the human.”

00:03:58.650 --> 00:04:02.239
Writing during the industrial revolution,
Bergson said when we laugh because someone

00:04:02.239 --> 00:04:06.719
falls, it’s not necessarily their misfortune
that we find funny, but the spectacle of a

00:04:06.719 --> 00:04:10.369
machine malfunctioning — in this case, our
bodies.

00:04:10.369 --> 00:04:15.699
For example: Take the classic prank of pulling
a chair out from under a friend about to sit.

00:04:15.699 --> 00:04:17.430
They fall on their ass.

00:04:17.430 --> 00:04:18.829
Hilarity ensues.

00:04:18.829 --> 00:04:22.790
According to Bergson, we laugh because an
action as routine as “sitting down” is

00:04:22.790 --> 00:04:25.590
basically the body on autopilot.

00:04:25.590 --> 00:04:29.870
Seeing that habitual motion fail is what generates
the lulz.

00:04:29.870 --> 00:04:34.260
And while this kind of falling-on-your-ass
humor certainly predates machines, we can

00:04:34.260 --> 00:04:38.759
still think of it in terms of mechanical rigidity
against human flexibility.

00:04:38.759 --> 00:04:43.930
It’s probably no coincidence that Bergson
was writing when factories and machines reshaped

00:04:43.930 --> 00:04:45.069
modern life.

00:04:45.069 --> 00:04:50.229
More than 30 years later, Charlie Chaplin
would, in some ways, embody elements of Bergson’s

00:04:50.229 --> 00:04:55.330
theory in “Modern Times,” where the intrusion
of mechanization onto the human is put front

00:04:55.330 --> 00:04:56.370
and center.

00:04:56.370 --> 00:05:01.009
If Modern Times was so popular, was it because
he struck a nerve with people’s relationship

00:05:01.009 --> 00:05:02.080
to machines?

00:05:02.080 --> 00:05:06.430
For Chaplin, it’s not just that humans are
acting like machines, but have also become

00:05:06.430 --> 00:05:09.320
entangled in a society of machines.

00:05:09.320 --> 00:05:14.229
Which brings us to modern day: we no longer
feel our sense of self threatened by physical

00:05:14.229 --> 00:05:16.199
machines, but another kind of machine.

00:05:16.199 --> 00:05:19.449
You see, Bergson’s formulation has evolved.

00:05:19.449 --> 00:05:24.240
Humor isn’t just the “machine encrusted
upon the human,” but “society encrusted

00:05:24.240 --> 00:05:25.240
upon the human.”

00:05:25.240 --> 00:05:30.190
The component parts of society: state and
corporate bureaucracy, cultural expectations,

00:05:30.190 --> 00:05:35.759
all act like a kind of machine - one that
happens to simultaneously feed us dreams and

00:05:35.759 --> 00:05:37.300
tear them away from us.

00:05:37.300 --> 00:05:41.901
The discrepancy between our fantasies that
helped us cope with reality, and the reality

00:05:41.901 --> 00:05:46.439
that forsakes those dreams is the new engine
of humor in 2018.

00:05:46.439 --> 00:05:52.340
Gary and His Demons imagines a “chosen”
demon hunter as a sad-sack working stiff who

00:05:52.340 --> 00:05:53.960
can’t even retire.

00:05:53.960 --> 00:05:56.050
“I realized why I'm mean all the time.

00:05:56.050 --> 00:05:59.909
It's because my job f**king sucks and my life
f**king sucks more than anyone else’s.

00:05:59.909 --> 00:06:01.419
And that's not navel-gazing.

00:06:01.419 --> 00:06:02.810
That is the truth.”

00:06:02.810 --> 00:06:06.289
Demons have to face being “in” or “out”
like fashion trends.

00:06:06.289 --> 00:06:09.229
“He's the hot new mirror monster.

00:06:09.229 --> 00:06:12.990
Runnin' his mouth about how the old guard
is out of touch.

00:06:12.990 --> 00:06:15.499
Bloody Mary dates back to the 1500s.

00:06:15.499 --> 00:06:17.599
I am mirror monsters!

00:06:17.599 --> 00:06:18.650
That’s me!”

00:06:18.650 --> 00:06:23.379
In Archer, even the exciting world of espionage
won’t take your mind off your flex account.

00:06:23.379 --> 00:06:27.650
"I forgot to spend the balance in my goddamn
flex account!"

00:06:27.650 --> 00:06:31.750
And while Rick may be the only one “above
it all” — I mean, he’s not bogged down

00:06:31.750 --> 00:06:33.990
by expense reports and bureaucracy, — “They’re
bureaucrats.

00:06:33.990 --> 00:06:35.490
I don’t respect them.

00:06:35.490 --> 00:06:36.979
Just keep shooting, Morty.”

00:06:36.979 --> 00:06:41.460
— his arch nemeses are a bunch of himselfs
that reduce other versions of himself to factory

00:06:41.460 --> 00:06:43.240
workers and stooges.

00:06:43.240 --> 00:06:47.719
The whole joke of “Simple Rick” is the
revealing the real nature of escapist fantasy

00:06:47.719 --> 00:06:52.930
as just another cog in the soulless machine
— “Come home to the unique flavor of shattering

00:06:52.930 --> 00:06:54.409
the grand illusion.

00:06:54.409 --> 00:06:55.569
Come home…

00:06:55.569 --> 00:06:56.870
to Simple Rick.”

00:06:56.870 --> 00:07:00.849
And it’s not just these three shows: this
kind of humor is everywhere.

00:07:00.849 --> 00:07:05.560
“The Good Place” imagines an afterlife
full of legal arbitration and algorithms.

00:07:05.560 --> 00:07:12.270
“When your time on earth has ended, we calculate
the total value of your life using our perfectly

00:07:12.270 --> 00:07:14.389
accurate measuring system.

00:07:14.389 --> 00:07:19.840
Only the people with the highest scores, the
true cream of the crop, get to come here to

00:07:19.840 --> 00:07:20.840
the good place.”

00:07:20.840 --> 00:07:26.389
“One Punch Man” imagines superhero-ing
as a dumb bureaucratic game mired in red tape,

00:07:26.389 --> 00:07:30.590
and the sleeper hit soon-to-be-TV-show “What
We Do In the Shadows” envisions frightening

00:07:30.590 --> 00:07:33.729
vampires fighting over banal roommate issues.

00:07:33.729 --> 00:07:35.590
“Vampires don’t do dishes!”

00:07:35.590 --> 00:07:39.570
To return to Bergson, if we find all this
funny, it’s because we are confronted with

00:07:39.570 --> 00:07:41.680
a mirror of our own lives.

00:07:41.680 --> 00:07:46.580
More than just laughing at bodies as machines,
we laugh when we imagine people as mere instruments

00:07:46.580 --> 00:07:48.349
of a greater machine.

00:07:48.349 --> 00:07:52.831
Bergson gives the example of a person who
performs their duty not like a human, but

00:07:52.831 --> 00:07:53.849
like a machine.

00:07:53.849 --> 00:07:58.400
He cites a story he found in a newspaper:
a large steamer was wrecked off the coast

00:07:58.400 --> 00:07:59.400
of France.

00:07:59.400 --> 00:08:04.720
After courageously rushing to save the passengers,
the customs officers didn’t ask if the survivors

00:08:04.720 --> 00:08:09.180
if they’re okay, but rather, immediately
asked if they had anything to declare.

00:08:09.180 --> 00:08:14.819
The customs officers were not acting as humans,
but mere instruments of a larger bureaucracy.

00:08:14.819 --> 00:08:19.550
And this is exactly what shows like Archer,
Gary and his Demons, and sometimes Rick and

00:08:19.550 --> 00:08:23.120
Morty, rely on — how we’ve become mere
instruments of a larger game.

00:08:23.120 --> 00:08:24.509
“We know how you feel.

00:08:24.509 --> 00:08:28.129
We’re working stiff Ricks just like you,
but our assembly line is justice.”

00:08:28.129 --> 00:08:32.400
It puts on display the incongruity between
the way we imagine the world ought to be,

00:08:32.400 --> 00:08:34.720
and the way it really is.

00:08:34.720 --> 00:08:38.849
Because of this discrepancy, we no longer
live in a world where we can stomach the cheery

00:08:38.849 --> 00:08:44.950
idealism of heroes past, but have to deconstruct
the fantasy at every turn because our fantasies

00:08:44.950 --> 00:08:46.420
have been deconstructed.

00:08:46.420 --> 00:08:51.070
We can’t imagine much of anything without
wanting to think of what is pulling its strings.

00:08:51.070 --> 00:08:56.240
Take the superhero one-liner, once a source
of catharsis — “Yippee kay-a motherf**ker.”

00:08:56.240 --> 00:08:59.260
It now needs to be exposed as a tired cliche.

00:08:59.260 --> 00:09:01.020
So we get this — “Table for one.”

00:09:01.020 --> 00:09:05.320
“Eh… you wanna try… you wanna try, eh…
one more time?”

00:09:05.320 --> 00:09:06.320
“Um…

00:09:06.320 --> 00:09:07.320
This guy's cut off.”

00:09:07.320 --> 00:09:09.529
— and this — “You should know this isn’t
personal.”

00:09:09.529 --> 00:09:11.510
“You should know that isn’t original.”

00:09:11.510 --> 00:09:15.260
—&nbsp;and this — “Gilette should be ready
with a transdermal solution of the most powerful

00:09:15.260 --> 00:09:16.860
laxative known to man.”

00:09:16.860 --> 00:09:17.860
“I…

00:09:17.860 --> 00:09:19.260
Dammit, I had something for this!”

00:09:19.260 --> 00:09:23.350
Marvel movies can’t sell superheroes without
constantly undercutting the seriousness of

00:09:23.350 --> 00:09:24.639
what they’re doing.

00:09:24.639 --> 00:09:25.639
“Hey!

00:09:25.639 --> 00:09:26.639
Hey!

00:09:26.639 --> 00:09:27.639
We know each other.

00:09:27.639 --> 00:09:29.410
He's a friend from work.”

00:09:29.410 --> 00:09:34.100
Deadpool is one of the most popular superheroes
by virtue of not being a hero at all.

00:09:34.100 --> 00:09:39.399
And things like CinemaSins draw humor from
subjecting films to a level of cold-hard logic

00:09:39.399 --> 00:09:41.590
that they weren’t even built for.

00:09:41.590 --> 00:09:45.529
This leads us to the second marker of modern
humor - a broken protagonist.

00:09:45.529 --> 00:09:49.810
Now, this certainly isn’t new — Jerry
Seinfeld did it over 20 years ago.

00:09:49.810 --> 00:09:54.350
But instead of looking up to Burt Reynolds,
we now admire a broken version of Burt Reynolds

00:09:54.350 --> 00:09:57.670
or a broken Van Helsing or a broken Doc Brown.

00:09:57.670 --> 00:10:01.680
And then there’s something like It’s Always
Sunny in Philadelphia, a show that, according

00:10:01.680 --> 00:10:06.500
to creator Glenn Howerton, shows what the
American Dream looks like when you fail.

00:10:06.500 --> 00:10:11.190
Even the notion of doing good deeds is shat
on: relishing in the collateral damage that

00:10:11.190 --> 00:10:13.959
often comes with being a “good guy.”

00:10:13.959 --> 00:10:19.350
Our demon hunter amputates an orphan by accident,
and tries to be a nicer person before realizing

00:10:19.350 --> 00:10:20.730
it’s pointless.

00:10:20.730 --> 00:10:22.120
“You came!

00:10:22.120 --> 00:10:24.149
I knew you cared about me!

00:10:24.149 --> 00:10:25.820
You ARE a good guy!”

00:10:25.820 --> 00:10:26.820
“F**k you!”

00:10:26.820 --> 00:10:30.850
Similarly, Morty tries to save an interdimensional
being from assassination only to discover

00:10:30.850 --> 00:10:35.899
it only wants to exterminate all life — “Carbon-based
life is a threat to all higher life.

00:10:35.899 --> 00:10:38.090
To us, you are what you would call a disease.”

00:10:38.090 --> 00:10:40.029
— and has to kill it himself.

00:10:40.029 --> 00:10:43.509
Rick highlights the futility of do-gooding
when he says, “What about the reality where

00:10:43.509 --> 00:10:44.509
Hitler cured cancer?

00:10:44.509 --> 00:10:46.449
The answer is don’t think about it.”

00:10:46.449 --> 00:10:51.330
And the entire premise of Archer is to explore
the self-obsessed eccentricities of the people

00:10:51.330 --> 00:10:54.540
who are supposed to keep us safe at night.

00:10:54.540 --> 00:10:55.639
“Rampaaaaaage!”

00:10:55.639 --> 00:11:01.880
To summarize: we were promised jetpacks and
hoverboards, and because we never got them:

00:11:01.880 --> 00:11:07.520
we have no fantasies, no heroes, and some
serious skepticism about doing good.

00:11:07.520 --> 00:11:11.900
And now the best sense of pleasure we can
get is collectively reflecting on that sense

00:11:11.900 --> 00:11:12.900
of disappointment.

00:11:12.900 --> 00:11:15.670
Now, that’s not to say we are completely
without dreams.

00:11:15.670 --> 00:11:19.500
Instead of dreaming of nicer cars and the
perfect relationship — which sure, definitely

00:11:19.500 --> 00:11:23.959
still happens — we also get gratification
by relishing in the utterly mundane.

00:11:23.959 --> 00:11:32.430
“Would you rather get embroiled in a particularly
nasty Ponzi scheme or go on a date with Trevor

00:11:32.430 --> 00:11:33.690
from school?!”

00:11:33.690 --> 00:11:37.990
Things like the Roy game or this are barely
even science fiction.

00:11:37.990 --> 00:11:40.300
“She’s obsessed with her new virtual bouncer
game.

00:11:40.300 --> 00:11:41.300
Real fun, ha ha ha.”

00:11:41.300 --> 00:11:42.300
“You on the list?

00:11:42.300 --> 00:11:43.300
Nice try, pal.

00:11:43.300 --> 00:11:44.570
Get to the back of the line!”

00:11:44.570 --> 00:11:49.220
Is it any different than that the wildly popular
Job Simulator game, where one pretends to

00:11:49.220 --> 00:11:52.779
be a robot poorly re-enacting mundane office
work?

00:11:52.779 --> 00:11:54.139
Or clicker games?

00:11:54.139 --> 00:11:55.980
But is everything hopeless?

00:11:55.980 --> 00:11:57.029
Of course not.

00:11:57.029 --> 00:12:01.700
Yes, we Wisecrackers can sometimes seem like
curmudgeons, but that’s what happens when

00:12:01.700 --> 00:12:03.560
you read philosophy, BRO!

00:12:03.560 --> 00:12:06.680
But I promise, there’s still hope - like
my dog, Woody.

00:12:06.680 --> 00:12:09.199
Look at that baby face muffin man.

00:12:09.199 --> 00:12:14.060
Oh, oh— or, for instance, I went to this
place that took my two favorite sauces - hummus

00:12:14.060 --> 00:12:17.250
and garlic sauce — and turned it into ONE
sauce.

00:12:17.250 --> 00:12:18.920
It pretty much shattered my worldview.

00:12:18.920 --> 00:12:23.600
So, look, how can you fixate on the hopelessness
of the modern condition when there are still

00:12:23.600 --> 00:12:26.639
breakthroughs made every day in sauce technology?

00:12:26.639 --> 00:12:31.120
And for more good ol’ fashioned distractions,
check out new episodes of Gary and his Demons

00:12:31.120 --> 00:12:33.940
on VRV every Sunday by going to vrv.co/wisecrack.

00:12:33.940 --> 00:12:39.690
Whether you’re online or offline you can
watch Gary and a bunch of other great animated

00:12:39.690 --> 00:12:44.740
shows on VRV: like Freakazoid, Bravest Warriors,
Deep Space 69, and Bee &amp; Puppycat.

00:12:44.740 --> 00:12:49.630
Also, if you're into anime, VRV has all the
best subbed AND dubbed shows, including: My

00:12:49.630 --> 00:12:53.540
Hero Academia, Dragon Ball Super, Black Clover,
and Food Wars.

00:12:53.540 --> 00:12:58.040
